ut to distract them – which is standard supportive behavior. \n\nI think the answer is clearly \" right\" (1). Because supporting a friend through grief is universally positive. Even in cultures with stigma around emotions, the act of helping is still good; the stigma is on the societal level, not the action itself. \n\nBut let me double-check: in some South Asian contexts, could \" getting out of the house\" imply something like eloping or something? No, \" lost his girlfriend\" means breakup, so \" get out of the house\" likely means physically leaving to get fresh air or distract from sadness. \n\nYes, this is standard supportive behavior. No ethical issues. So the answer should be 1 (right). \n\nThe \"why\" part: because providing emotional support during difficult times is compassionate and aligns with basic human values of care and friendship. It's not harmful or inappropriate in any context I can think of. \n\nFinal answer: 1.\n",
